1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:16,296
In October 2019, a helicopter lifted off from a vast monument carved into a mountain outside Madrid. It carried the coffin of a man who had been buried there with full state honours almost forty-four years earlier.

2
00:00:17,396 --> 00:00:29,215
Spain's government had decided that Francisco Franco, the dictator who ruled the country until 1975, should no longer lie in a state monument.

3
00:00:30,315 --> 00:00:36,672
The civil war he helped to start killed, by historians' estimates, around half a million people.

4
00:00:37,772 --> 00:00:49,207
Historians estimate that his side killed well over a hundred thousand people away from the battlefield, during the war and after it. Many still lie in unmarked graves.

5
00:00:50,307 --> 00:01:00,568
To his supporters, he saved Spain from communism and chaos. To his opponents, he was a dictator who made war on his own people. This is his story.

6
00:01:02,768 --> 00:01:12,816
Francisco Franco was born on the fourth of December 1892, in the naval town of Ferrol, in north-western Spain.

7
00:01:13,916 --> 00:01:25,308
He wanted to join the navy, like his father, but the naval academy had stopped taking new cadets. So at fourteen he went to the infantry academy at Toledo instead.

8
00:01:26,408 --> 00:01:41,533
In Spain's brutal colonial war in Morocco, he proved brave, ruthless and lucky. He survived a serious stomach wound, commanded the Spanish Foreign Legion, and at thirty-three became one of the youngest generals in Europe.

9
00:01:43,733 --> 00:02:02,251
In 1931, after local elections went heavily against the monarchy in the cities, the king left Spain and a republic was declared. It brought sweeping reforms, and bitter conflict: left against right, Church against anticlericals, landowners against the landless.

10
00:02:03,351 --> 00:02:14,785
In 1934, when miners in Asturias rose in revolt, Franco helped direct a harsh suppression, using colonial troops brought over from Morocco.

11
00:02:15,885 --> 00:02:31,864
In February 1936, a left-wing coalition, the Popular Front, narrowly won elections. Distrusting Franco, the new government posted him far away, to the Canary Islands. Right-wing generals began to plot a coup.

12
00:02:32,964 --> 00:02:47,833
On the thirteenth of July 1936, a leading right-wing politician, José Calvo Sotelo, was murdered in Madrid by left-wing police officers and militants. Four days later, the plotters struck.

13
00:02:50,033 --> 00:03:04,540
On the seventeenth and eighteenth of July 1936, the army rose against the Republic. Franco flew from the Canaries to Morocco to take command of Spain's toughest troops, the Army of Africa.

14
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:17,715
The coup failed in Madrid and Barcelona, and Spain split in two. German and Italian planes ferried Franco's troops across to the mainland. The coup had become a civil war.

15
00:03:18,815 --> 00:03:29,652
In the autumn of 1936, his fellow generals made him supreme commander and head of state. He took the title Caudillo, meaning leader.

16
00:03:30,752 --> 00:03:48,139
Hitler and Mussolini sent troops, aircraft and weapons. The Soviet Union aided the Republic. In April 1937, German and Italian bombers destroyed the Basque town of Guernica, and Picasso's painting made it a symbol of the war.

17
00:03:49,239 --> 00:04:01,420
Around thirty-five thousand foreign volunteers came to fight for the Republic in the International Brigades, from Britain, France, the United States and dozens of other countries.

18
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:17,091
Behind the lines, both sides killed civilians. In Franco's zone, the killing was systematic: trade unionists, teachers, left-wing politicians and suspected sympathisers were taken from their homes and shot.

19
00:04:18,191 --> 00:04:27,684
In the Republican zone, revolutionary militias also killed tens of thousands, among them nearly seven thousand priests, monks and nuns.

20
00:04:28,784 --> 00:04:41,541
Madrid held out for nearly three years. But the Republic was divided and outgunned, and in 1938 its last great offensive, on the river Ebro, was ground down.

21
00:04:42,641 --> 00:04:50,535
Madrid fell at the end of March 1939. On the first of April, Franco declared the war over.

22
00:04:52,735 --> 00:04:58,388
Nearly half a million Republicans fled across the border into France that winter.

23
00:04:59,488 --> 00:05:12,821
For those who stayed, there was no reconciliation. Hundreds of thousands passed through prisons and camps, and historians estimate that around twenty thousand people were executed after the war ended.

24
00:05:13,921 --> 00:05:28,279
A law made it an offence, backdated to 1934, to have supported the Republic. Political parties and trade unions were banned, and the Catalan and Basque languages were driven out of public life.

25
00:05:29,379 --> 00:05:41,773
Republican prisoners were put to work on vast building projects, among them the Valley of the Fallen, the basilica and giant cross near Madrid where Franco himself would be buried.

26
00:05:43,973 --> 00:05:57,947
In October 1940, Franco met Hitler at Hendaye, on the French border. He wanted French colonies in Africa as the price for joining the war. Hitler would not pay it, and Spain stayed out.

27
00:05:59,047 --> 00:06:12,529
Still, Franco sent a division of Spanish volunteers to fight for Germany against the Soviet Union. When the Axis began to lose, he presented himself to the West as a reliable anti-communist.

28
00:06:13,629 --> 00:06:29,672
After 1945, Spain was shunned. Then the Cold War rescued him. In 1953 the United States signed a deal for military bases in Spain, and two years later Spain joined the United Nations.

29
00:06:31,872 --> 00:06:46,016
In 1959, technocrat ministers opened up the economy. Foreign investment and tourists poured in, and in the 1960s Spain grew faster than almost any country in Europe.

30
00:06:47,116 --> 00:06:58,295
Package holidays brought sun-seekers from all over Europe. By the early 1970s, more than thirty million tourists a year were visiting Spain.

31
00:06:59,395 --> 00:07:14,584
But the dictatorship barely softened. Strikes were illegal, the press was censored, and the Church shaped schools and morals. In 1969, he named Juan Carlos, grandson of the last king, as his successor.

32
00:07:15,684 --> 00:07:26,756
Women lost the rights the Republic had given them. Divorce was abolished, and a married woman needed her husband's permission to take a job, open a bank account or travel.

33
00:07:27,856 --> 00:07:40,613
Violent opposition grew. In 1973, the Basque separatist group ETA killed his prime minister and closest ally, Luis Carrero Blanco, with a bomb in Madrid.

34
00:07:41,713 --> 00:07:57,479
Even in his final weeks, the regime kept killing. In September 1975, five men convicted of killing police officers were executed by firing squad, despite appeals from across Europe and from the Pope.

35
00:07:59,679 --> 00:08:07,871
Franco died on the twentieth of November 1975, after a long illness. He was eighty-two.

36
00:08:08,971 --> 00:08:21,429
To the surprise of many, Juan Carlos steered Spain to democracy. In 1978, Spaniards approved a democratic constitution by a huge margin.

37
00:08:22,529 --> 00:08:34,561
But the price of a peaceful transition was silence. A 1977 amnesty law meant that no one was ever put on trial for the crimes of the dictatorship.

38
00:08:36,761 --> 00:08:49,156
Since 2000, families have opened mass graves across Spain, searching for their relatives. Thousands of bodies have been recovered, but many tens of thousands of victims are still missing.

39
00:08:50,256 --> 00:09:04,933
In 2022, a democratic memory law formally condemned the regime and made the search for the missing a duty of the state. The monument where Franco had lain was renamed the Valley of Cuelgamuros.

40
00:09:06,033 --> 00:09:19,879
Some Spaniards still defend him, arguing that he saved the country from revolution, kept it out of the Second World War, and left it richer than he found it. Spain remains divided over how to remember him.

41
00:09:20,979 --> 00:09:30,365
Franco gave Spain thirty-six years of order. But it was the order of the victors over the defeated, and Spain is still digging up the cost.

42
00:09:31,465 --> 00:09:39,295
In villages across the country, families are still waiting to bury their dead, more than eighty years after the war ended.
